From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PS3 Linux project <ps3@localhost>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 21:03:40 +0300
Subject: GL: fewer, larger draws (batched walls, flats, sprites and HUD
 patches)

The GL renderer drew every wall as an immediate-mode fan, every flat loop with its own
glDrawArrays (after a matrix push and translation per flat), every sprite and every
status bar or HUD patch as an immediate-mode strip, and set the colour and the light
level uniform before each of them. Freedoom's DEMO1 timedemo at 1920x1080 made ~1960 GL
calls and ~205 draws a frame; on a CPU-bound GL 2.1 driver (Mesa nv30 on the PS3) each
draw costs state validation and submission.

gl_batch.c collects the walls, flats and opaque sprites of each pass of gld_DrawScene
(floors, ceilings, walls, mid walls, sprites) into one client vertex array as
GL_TRIANGLES, the fans and strips decomposed the way Mesa does (same triangles, same
vertex order), the flat loops raised to their height on the CPU (from a copy of the
flats VBO data). The pending triangles are drawn when the state they depend on changes.
gl_batchflush.h, included last by every source that calls GL, makes each GL call that
draws or changes state draw them first, so the order of everything stays as before.
Draws that aren't batched (flooded walls, fuzz sprites, flats with a texture transform)
break the batch. gld_StaticLightAlpha skips unchanged colours and light levels.

The light level travels per vertex (the attribute a_light, the main shader built with
VLIGHT; draws that aren't batched set its current value), so walls and flats of
different sectors batch together. HUD patches (status bar, fonts; indexed, up to 320x64)
come from one 512x512 atlas texture, each framed by a copy of its edge texels, so a
status bar becomes about two draws.

On the host (llvmpipe forced to GL 2.1 without ARB_texture_rg) the timedemo makes ~390
GL calls and ~46 draws a frame. The scene is the same but for up to ~50 pixels a frame
on polygon and light band edges; the atlas changes ~5000 HUD pixels, where a pixel
centre falls exactly on a texel edge of the 4.5x stretched status bar (ties that the
rounding of the texture coordinates decides).

Run-time switches, all on unless set to 0: DSDA_GL_BATCH (walls and sprites),
DSDA_GL_FLATS, DSDA_GL_HUD (the atlas), DSDA_GL_VLIGHT; with all four 0 the output is
the old renderer's, pixel for pixel. DSDA_GL_STATS=1 logs the batched draws and items
per frame every 350 frames.

Forwarded: no

